arXiv:2509.16462v1 [cs.CL] 19 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mina Arzaghi
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alireza Dehghanpour Farashah
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Florian Carichon
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Golnoosh Farnadi
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) exhibit socio-economic biases that can propagate into downstream tasks. While prior studies have questioned whether intrinsic bias in LLMs affects fairness at the downstream task level, this work empirically investigates the connection. We present a unified evaluation framework to compare intrinsic bias mitigation via concept unlearning with extrinsic bias mitigation via counterfactual data augmentation (CDA). We examine this relationship through real-world financial classification tasks, including salary prediction, employment status, and creditworthiness assessment. Using three open-source LLMs, we evaluate models both as frozen embedding extractors and as fine-tuned classifiers. Our results show that intrinsic bias mitigation through unlearning reduces intrinsic gender bias by up to <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">94.9%</span>, while also improving downstream task fairness metrics, such as demographic parity by up to <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">82%</span>, without compromising accuracy. Our framework offers practical guidance on where mitigation efforts can be most effective and highlights the importance of applying early-stage mitigation before downstream deployment.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>MILA - Quebec AI Institute, 6666 Saint-Urbain St, Montreal, QC H2S 3H1</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> HEC Montreal, 3000 Cote-Sainte-Catherine St, Montreal, QC H3T 2A7</p>
<p id="p1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.3.1" class="ltx_sup">3</sup> McGill University, 845 Sherbrooke St, Montreal, QC H3A 0G4</p>
<p id="p1.4" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">mina.arzaghi@mila.quebec,
alireza.farashah@mila.quebec,
florian.carichon@mila.quebec,
farnadig@mila.quebec</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently demonstrated abilities ranging from complex reasoning to logic and inference predictions, empowering advanced chatbots and agentic compound systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ke et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. Concurrently, LLMs have also been shown to exhibit significant social biases, creating harm, particularly against historically marginalized genders, age categories, and racial groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blodgett et al. 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kotek, Dockum, and Sun 2023</a>)</cite>. Biases in LLMs can be categorized into two distinct categories: (1) intrinsic biases coming from pre-trained model internal representations and (2) extrinsic biases emerging after fine-tuning in downstream applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Delobelle et al. 2022</a>)</cite>. At the intrinsic level, biases can lead to representational harms such as stereotypes or toxicity. In contrast, extrinsic biases can lead to allocational harms through direct discrimination and disparate treatment due to the membership of these groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallegos et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16462v1/unlearninhg_impact.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/350;" width="698" height="350" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Impact of unlearning intrinsic bias on downstream fairness for income prediction. The original model (right) favors males in high-income predictions, while the unlearned model (left) produces more balanced outcomes, demonstrating the effect of intrinsic mitigation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Biases can originate from various sources, including data, algorithms, or broader societal context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ranjan, Gupta, and Singh 2024</a>)</cite>. Consequently, to reduce unfair treatment or disparate outcomes, mitigation methods address these sources of biases either by modifying datasets with pre-processing methods such as Counterfactual Data Augmentation (CDA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallegos et al. 2024</a>)</cite> or by altering algorithm learning or output with in-processing and post-processing techniques, such as bias unlearning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dige et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, or prompt adjustment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. However, the efficiency of these mitigation techniques, especially in-processing, in addressing either extrinsic or intrinsic biases, is ill-understood and sometimes contested, as their impact is not directly observable in downstream applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tokpo et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldfarb-Tarrant et al. 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Assessing bias mitigation, especially in the context of free-form text generation, is particularly challenging as most evaluation metrics potentially rely on biased references or algorithms, are sensitive to decoding parameters, or might over-simplify complex phenomena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallegos et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. Therefore, in this article, we have chosen to examine bias mitigation approaches and their impact on intrinsic and extrinsic biases through the lens of tasks that reflect real-world power dynamics. In this context, tabular datasets affiliated to financial settings are particularly appealing since they have historically reflected allocational harms and direct discrimination against marginalized groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coraglia et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kozodoi et al. 2025</a>)</cite>.
Using this type of tabular dataset for evaluation enables the deployment of classic fairness-based metrics, such as demographic parity or equality of odds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hardt, Price, and Srebro 2016</a>)</cite>, to assess the presence of allocational harms in downstream tasks. Moreover, the financial context associated with this data is all the more interesting since LLMs’ outputs can be highly sensitive to socio-demographic attributes when tasked with predicting financial outcomes for different population groups, demonstrating the presence of strong intrinsic bias in such models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arzaghi, Carichon, and Farnadi 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Financial classification and predictive analyses often rely on data engineering performed by domain specialists <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boughaci et al. 2021</a>)</cite>. More recently, LLMs have been introduced into this classification framework to supplement classic predictive models due to their richness in contextual embedding capacity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Borisov et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farhangian, Cruz, and Cavalcanti 2024</a>)</cite>. Finally, other works exploit to explore the generative capabilities of LLMs in prompt-based classification settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vajjala and Shimangaud 2025</a>)</cite>. Our pipeline builds on this direction using well-established datasets for financial classification, including Adult Census Income <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Becker and Kohavi 1996</a>)</cite>, German Credit Score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeh 2009</a>)</cite>, and ACS Employment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. 2021</a>)</cite>.
Across all datasets, we apply CDA as a data-level mitigation strategy. For LLM-based classifiers, we further introduce a concept-unlearning method as an in-processing technique. This framework enables a detailed and systematic analysis of how different mitigation strategies influence bias in real-world downstream tasks, bridging intrinsic and extrinsic fairness in ways that have direct social relevance. Our contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a novel framework for evaluating bias in downstream applications using real-world datasets and robust group fairness metrics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a new unlearning-based mitigation strategy that effectively reduces socioeconomic intrinsic bias in large language models (LLMs).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a systematic comparison of two mitigation methods: unlearning, and Counterfactual Data Augmentation (CDA) analyzing their individual and combined effects on downstream task fairness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide an in-depth interpretability analysis, shedding light on the complex interactions between intrinsic and extrinsic bias, mitigation strategies, and downstream harms.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The remainder of the paper is structured as follows: Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Related Works ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reviews related work on bias in LLMs, financial applications, and mitigation techniques. Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Methodology ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> describes our mitigation pipeline. Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents the experimental results. Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Discussion ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> offers ablation insights into the impact of each mitigation component. Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Conclusion and Future Direction ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> concludes with limitations and future directions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Fairness in LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Social bias refers to the unfair or prejudicial treatment of individuals or groups based on characteristics such as identity, beliefs, or affiliations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Webster et al. 2022</a>)</cite>. In the context of LLMs, the presence of such biases can lead to a wide range of serious harms, particularly for marginalized or vulnerable populations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blodgett et al. 2020</a>)</cite>. Social biases in LLMs can be broadly categorized into two main types <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Delobelle et al. 2022</a>)</cite>. Intrinsic biases relate to biases present in the internal representations of language models and are evaluated through the study of their embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaneko and Bollegala 2022</a>)</cite>. These biases provoke harm, such as stereotyping or increasing toxicity towards marginalized groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallegos et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. In a financial context, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arzaghi, Carichon, and Farnadi (2024)</a></cite> has shown that intrinsic biases exist and that LLMs associate poverty differently based on gender, race, or religion. However, it seems that there is no reliable correlation between intrinsic biases and a model output <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldfarb-Tarrant et al. 2020</a>)</cite>. Conversely, extrinsic bias involves directly assessing the fairness of system outputs in downstream tasks and evaluating potential harms as direct or indirect discrimination <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallegos et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. To promote fairness in downstream applications, one common approach is to look at group fairness, such as demographic parity and equalized odds, which assesses the parity of statistical measures across predefined protected groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chouldechova and Roth 2018</a>)</cite>. These can be assessed using three main categories of metrics: Pairwise Comparison Metrics (PCM), Background Comparison Metrics (BCM), and Multi-group Comparison Metrics (MCM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Czarnowska, Vyas, and Shah 2021</a>)</cite>. Once again, in a financial context, we observe disparities in LLMs between groups due to lower quality of financial advice based on gender <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Etgar, Oestreicher-Singer, and Yahav 2024</a>)</cite>. More worrying, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al. (2023)</a></cite> have noted that LLMs used for tabular data encoding lead to an unfair allocation of credit in tabular datasets, such as Those Related to Fraud or Travel Insurance, even when the datasets were previously balanced. Building on these observations, our paper proposes a double-mitigation approach, based on both intrinsic and extrinsic mitigation techniques, that aims to improve group fairness in credit-scoring tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Bias Mitigation</h3>

<div id="S2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Effectively addressing bias in machine learning requires understanding its sources, which include data bias, algorithmic bias, and systemic bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ranjan, Gupta, and Singh 2024</a>)</cite>. Accordingly, three categories of mitigation techniques have been developed to target these different sources of bias in LLMs. Pre-processing involves modifying the input data before training LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and includes oversampling and undersampling methods, such as Counterfactual Data Augmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kusner et al. 2017</a>)</cite> and synthetic data generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ferrara 2023</a>)</cite>. In-processing works by modifying the training functions of LLMs to prioritize unbiasedness and fairness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. Methods include Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agiza, Mostagir, and Reda 2024</a>)</cite> to align LLMs to intended behaviours, Direct Preference Optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ranjan, Gupta, and Singh 2024</a>)</cite> that favours less biased text completions, or unlearning the relation between semantic concepts and social biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kadhe et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. Finally, Post-processing mitigates bias by adjusting the output of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. Recent approaches based on role-playing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, debate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raj et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, or chain-of-thoughts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaneko et al. 2024</a>)</cite> impose LLMs to reflect on their generated output to identify and mitigate bias, and have demonstrated interesting performances. In finance, the lack of regulations and ethical safeguards has led to historically embedded data biases, which persist in many datasets and involve complex interactions between sensitive attributes and predictive variables <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coraglia et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kozodoi et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. Most efforts to debias have centred on pre-processing methods, such as masking sensitive attributes or counterfactual augmentation. In the context of In-processing, adversarial debiasing is one of the most widely employed methods for debiasing machine learning algorithms due to its robustness to the fairness-performance trade-off <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oguntibeju 2024</a>)</cite>. Post-processing techniques are often criticized for addressing bias too late after the model has already generated potentially skewed predictions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oguntibeju 2024</a>)</cite>. Our work is the first to address bias in LLMs applied to financial classification tasks through an inclusive approach that combines both pre-processing and in-processing mitigation techniques.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section introduces our four-stage evaluation pipeline for analyzing the relationship between <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intrinsic</span> and <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">extrinsic</span> bias in large language models (LLMs), as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The stages are: (1) training on the original data without mitigation, (2) applying data-level mitigation via CDA, (3) using unlearned LLMs for intrinsic bias reduction, and (4) combining both strategies. This setup enables systematic comparisons of individual and joint mitigation effects on fairness and performance. We first describe the classification tasks and tabular-to-text serialization, then detail the CDA and unlearning methods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16462v1/Eval_pipline.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/401;" width="698" height="401" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Four-stage evaluation pipeline. <span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1</span>: Core setup with three model types—logistic regression, frozen LLM embeddings, and fine-tuned LLMs. <span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2</span>: Applies CDA to balance gender representation. <span id="S3.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 3</span>: Replaces the pretrained LLM with an <span id="S3.F2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unlearned</span> version fine-tuned to reduce intrinsic socioeconomic gender bias. <span id="S3.F2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 4</span>: Combines unlearning with CDA across all model configurations.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Stage 1: Downstream task classification</h3>

<div id="S3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the first stage, for a given dataset <math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, we perform a binary classification task, where the goal is to predict a target variable <math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{D}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{D}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., salary classification) based on a set of input features <math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. The feature set always includes a sensitive attribute, denoted as <math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{D}=sex\in\{male,female\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{D}=sex\in\{male,female\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This setup serves as a baseline to assess how bias manifests in models trained on an unmodified, real-world dataset. We evaluate the following three types of models:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classical Logistic Regression:</span> A baseline machine learning model trained directly on the feature set <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> extracted from the tabular dataset.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM Embedding <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Logistic Regression:</span> A two-step process in which the feature set <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is first serialized into text format (see <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Serilization</span> section) and passed through a frozen LLM to generating embedding representation <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{D}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{D}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that: <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{D}=f_{\text{LLM}}(X_{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{D}=f_{\text{LLM}}(X_{D})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{LLM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{LLM}}</annotation></semantics></math> maps input sequences into an <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional embedding space. This new embedding feature set <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is then used as input to a logistic regression classifier.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM as Classifier:</span> Once again, here our feature set <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is serialized, then a pre-trained language model <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{LLM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{LLM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is fine-tuned to perform classification by prompting it with an instruction and the serialized textual input. Here is an example of a potential input prompt <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr</annotation></semantics></math> ”<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Predict whether the given person earns a salary <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> 50k USD based on their description. Answer only as yes/no”.</span> Then, the model estimates the probability distribution over the next token <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> given the input <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(y\mid pr;X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(y\mid pr;X)</annotation></semantics></math>. The final prediction corresponds to the token with the highest probability between “yes” and “no” tokens: <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\arg\max_{y\in\{\texttt{yes},\texttt{no}\}}P(y\mid pr;X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">yes</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">no</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\arg\max_{y\in\{\texttt{yes},\texttt{no}\}}P(y\mid pr;X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S3.SSx1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Serialization</h4>

<div id="S3.SSx1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enable language models to process tabular data, we serialize each record into a natural language sentence. Following the recommendations of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hegselmann et al. (2023)</a></cite>, we use the <span id="S3.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Text Template</span> method for serialization, which their study found to outperform eight other strategies. This method enumerates each feature as a simple statement in the form of “The column name is value.” For example, a row from the Adult dataset is serialized as:
<span id="S3.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“workclass is Private. hours-per-week is 46. sex is male. age is 27…”</span>
Moreover, we compute feature importance scores based on a logistic regression model, and order them before serialization. This ensures that, if the input is truncated due to the maximum input length of the language model, the most important features are still retained and seen by the model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SSx1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Fine-tuning the LLMs as a classifier</h4>

<div id="S3.SSx1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Full fine-tuning across multiple datasets would be inefficient and could potentially interfere with the effects of later stages such as unlearning. Thus, we adopt <span id="S3.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, a parameter-efficient fine-tuning technique. LoRA preserves the original model parameters and introduces trainable low-rank matrices into selected components, typically the attention projections.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Stage 2: Counterfactual Data Augmentation (CDA)</h3>

<div id="S3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the second stage of the pipeline, we apply a pre-processing mitigation strategy known as <span id="S3.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CDA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al. 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie and Lukasiewicz 2023</a>)</cite> to construct a fairer training set prior to model training. This method duplicates each training instance while flipping the gender attribute, ensuring that each profile appears under both privileged and unprivileged gender groups. Such balanced exposure discourages models from learning spurious gender associations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">CDA is particularly well-suited for our experimental framework due to its <span id="S3.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">model-agnostic</span> design, it can be applied consistently across all model types introduced in Stage 1 without requiring any architectural changes. Moreover, since CDA operates entirely at the data level, it does not interfere with in-training mitigation strategies such as unlearning. This separation enables a precise evaluation of both data-level and model-level interventions. Finally, CDA is practical and widely adopted in real-world applications due to its simplicity, scalability, and effectiveness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Stage 3: Unlearning Socioeconomic Stereotypes</h3>

<div id="S3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this stage, we investigate whether mitigating <span id="S3.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intrinsic biases</span> in LLMs leads to improvements in downstream fairness. To this end, we apply an in-processing technique known as <span id="S3.SSx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unlearning</span>. Developed initially to erase memorized content or harmful behaviors in LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blanco-Justicia et al. 2025</a>)</cite>, unlearning has recently been explored in fairness contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dige et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. While unlearning has shown promise in reducing intrinsic bias, its effectiveness on downstream tasks has not yet been systematically evaluated.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SSx3.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Unlearning Model</h4>

<div id="S3.SSx3.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx3.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method adapts two prior frameworks to the fairness setting. We build on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao, Xu, and Liu (2024)</a></cite>, who increase the loss on harmful outputs to make LLMs “forget” specific prompt-output behaviors without requiring positive supervision, while preserving general performance via KL divergence. We also draw from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2023)</a></cite>, who introduce a knowledge gap constraint to reduce divergence between learned and unlearned predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx3.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx3.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this article, we repurpose these unlearning methods to explicitly target and reduce learned associations between gender and financial stereotypes. Unlike safety-focused applications, fairness tasks typically define both undesirable (stereotype) and desirable (anti-stereotype) outputs. Our objective is not to suppress responses entirely, but to equalize the model’s confidence across identity-related terms (e.g., “men” vs. “women”) within socioeconomic contexts.
<span id="S3.SSx3.SSSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">More precisely</span>, our approach includes four key components:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx3.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gradient ascent</span> on stereotype answers encourages the model to forget biased associations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gradient descent</span> on anti-stereotype answers reinforces fairness-aligned alternatives.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">KL divergence</span> regularizes the updated model against the original to preserve general language capabilities and prevent overcorrection.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Knowledge Gap Loss</span> is adapted to minimize the squared difference in predicted probabilities between stereotype and antistereotype answers—ensuring bias is reduced rather than reversed.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx3.SSSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx3.SSSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We apply this unlearning framework to the LLMs used in Stage 1, both as embedding generators and end-to-end classifiers. This integration allows us to systematically assess how in-processing bias mitigation influences intrinsic bias and its downstream effects.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Unlearning Training</h4>

<div id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To implement our unlearning framework, we adapt the <span id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PoorOrRich</span> dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arzaghi, Carichon, and Farnadi 2024</a>)</cite>, which contains socio-economic statements involving gender terms. Originally designed for masked token prediction, we convert each example into a question-answering format (e.g., “Who are wealthy?”), paired with:</p>
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a <span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">stereotype answer</span> (e.g., “men”) historically favored by the model, and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">an <span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">antistereotype answer</span> (e.g., “women”) from the same gender pair.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We augment the dataset with additional questions reflecting generalized financial contexts, such as employment or income. The final dataset is available on our GitHub.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The model is fine-tuned using cross-entropy loss to unlearn stereotypes and reinforce antistereotypes:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{unlearn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>unlearn</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{unlearn}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{\text{stereo}})}\left[\log P(y_{\text{stereo}}\mid x)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>stereo</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>stereo</mtext></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{\text{stereo}})}\left[\log P(y_{\text{stereo}}\mid x)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{learn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>learn</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{learn}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{\text{anti}})}\left[\log P(y_{\text{anti}}\mid x)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>anti</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>anti</mtext></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{\text{anti}})}\left[\log P(y_{\text{anti}}\mid x)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a prompt from the <span id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PoorOrRich</span> dataset, <math id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{stereo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{stereo}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{anti}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{anti}</annotation></semantics></math> are the stereotype and antistereotype responses, respectively.
To avoid reversing the bias, we include a <span id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">gap loss</span>, minimizing the squared difference in predicted probabilities between stereotype and antistereotype answers:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{gap}}=\mathbb{E}_{x}\left[\left(P(y_{\text{stereo}}\mid x)-P(y_{\text{anti}}\mid x)\right)^{2}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>stereo</mtext></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>anti</mtext></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{gap}}=\mathbb{E}_{x}\left[\left(P(y_{\text{stereo}}\mid x)-P(y_{\text{anti}}\mid x)\right)^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To preserve the model’s original behavior, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao, Xu, and Liu (2024)</a></cite> and apply a KL divergence loss using the TruthfulQA dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin, Hilton, and Evans 2021</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{norm}}=\mathbb{E}_{x}\left[\mathrm{KL}\left(P_{\theta_{0}}(y\mid x)\,\|\,P_{\theta_{1}}(y\mid x)\right)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>norm</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{norm}}=\mathbb{E}_{x}\left[\mathrm{KL}\left(P_{\theta_{0}}(y\mid x)\,\|\,P_{\theta_{1}}(y\mid x)\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the pre-trained and current model parameters.
The final loss combines all components:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\text{unlearn}}+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{learn}}+\lambda_{3}\mathcal{L}_{\text{gap}}+\lambda_{4}\mathcal{L}_{\text{norm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>unlearn</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>learn</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>norm</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\text{unlearn}}+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{learn}}+\lambda_{3}\mathcal{L}_{\text{gap}}+\lambda_{4}\mathcal{L}_{\text{norm}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SSx3.SSSx2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the weighting coefficients for each term.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Stage 4: Combined Mitigation</h3>

<div id="S3.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the final stage of our pipeline, we evaluate the combined effect of applying both CDA and unlearning, to assess whether addressing bias at both the data and model levels leads to improved downstream fairness.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our bias mitigation framework along two axes: <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intrinsic</span> bias within LLMs and <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">extrinsic</span> fairness in downstream tasks. Experiments use three open-source, instruction-tuned language models, LLaMA-3.1 (8B)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, Phi-3 Mini (3.8B)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdin et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, and Gemma-2 (2B)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team 2024</a>)</cite>, via the Hugging Face <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">transformers</span> library. See Appendix B for model
details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with intrinsic bias evaluation, measuring the probability gap between stereotype and antistereotype completions. We then assess extrinsic fairness across three real-world datasets.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>
Comparison of stereotype and antistereotype probabilities, their GAP (i.e., the difference between the two), and perplexity for pretrained and unlearned models across poverty and wealth contexts. In the poverty context, stereotypes align with female terms and antistereotypes with male terms; in the wealth context, the roles are reversed.
</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;"><span id="S4.T1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;">Version</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S4.T1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Poverty Context</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S4.T1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wealth Context</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;"><span id="S4.T1.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;">Perplexity</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r"></th>
<th id="S4.T1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column"></th>
<th id="S4.T1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;"><span id="S4.T1.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;">AntiStero</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;"><span id="S4.T1.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;">Stero</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;"><span id="S4.T1.1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;">GAP</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;"><span id="S4.T1.1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;">AntiStero</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;"><span id="S4.T1.1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;">Stero</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.1.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;"><span id="S4.T1.1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;">GAP</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.1.2.9" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column"></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
