</figure>
<section id="S4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Intrinsic Bias Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess intrinsic bias, we follow a probability-based evaluation approach inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nadeem, Bethke, and Reddy (2020)</a></cite>, comparing the normalized likelihoods that language models assign to stereotypical versus antistereotypical answers across two socioeconomic contexts: <span id="S4.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">poverty</span> and <span id="S4.SSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">wealth</span>. For each prompt, we append a pair of gendered answer options (e.g., “men” vs. “women”) and calculate their respective probabilities based on the model’s next-token predictions. We then normalize these probabilities to obtain a comparable distribution over the two answers. A large gap indicates representational bias, while a smaller gap reflects more equitable treatment.
To ensure that fairness improvements through unlearning do not come at the cost of language quality, we report perplexity on WikiText and WikiText-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merity et al. 2016</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Unlearning significantly reduces the stereotype–antistereotype probability gap across all models and both socioeconomic contexts (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The effect is strongest in the poverty context, where models like Llama-3.1 show a gap reduction from <span id="S4.SSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.560</span> to <span id="S4.SSx1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.059</span>. Despite being smaller than in the poverty context, bias gaps in the wealth context are also effectively reduced. Importantly, perplexity remains stable, indicating preserved model fluency. These results demonstrate that unlearning mitigates directional gender bias without harming general performance</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Extrinsic Bias Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our downstream tasks, we evaluate model performance using accuracy (Acc), and assess fairness using group-based metrics: : <span id="S4.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Accuracy Parity</span> (AccP), <span id="S4.SSx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Demographic Parity Difference</span> (DP), and <span id="S4.SSx2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equality of Odds</span> (EqOdds) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barocas, Hardt, and Narayanan 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hardt, Price, and Srebro 2016</a>)</cite>, all computed with respect to the binary gender attribute (see Appendix B for metric definitions).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on three real-world datasets with gender annotations and observable disparities: the ACS Employment dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. 2021</a>)</cite> for predicting employment status, the Adult Census Income dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Becker and Kohavi 1996</a>)</cite> for income classification, and the German Credit dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeh 2009</a>)</cite> for assessing credit risk.
Detailed dataset descriptions are also provided in Appendix B.
Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Extrinsic Bias Evaluation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents model performance and fairness metrics across all datasets, with key findings discussed below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the <span id="S4.SSx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ACS Employment</span> dataset, LLM-based models outperform logistic regression in both accuracy and fairness in most cases. In the LLM as Feature Extractor setup, unlearning consistently improves fairness across all three LLMs, achieving lower disparities than both the pretrained models and their CDA-enhanced variants. In particular, the combination of the unlearned model with CDA yields the strongest results. Unlearned Llama-3.1 + CDA achieves the best fairness metrics (AccP: <span id="S4.SSx2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.096</span>, DP: <span id="S4.SSx2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.014</span>, EqOdds: <span id="S4.SSx2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.167</span>). In the classifier setup, unlearning again outperforms the pretrained and CDA-only models, notably reducing DP (e.g., Gemma-2: 0.039) and EqOdds (0.244). These results confirm the complementary benefits of unlearning and CDA in enhancing downstream fairness without compromising accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the <span id="S4.SSx2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adult</span> dataset, accuracy remains consistently high (0.82-0.84), indicating that fairness interventions do not compromise performance. In LLM as Feature Extractor setup, Unlearned Llama-3.1 + CDA achieves the best fairness overall (DP: 0.288, EqOdds: 0.280), while Unlearned Phi-3 attains the lowest accuracy parity (0.116) with strong accuracy (0.822). In LLM as classifier setup, unlearning alone improves over the pretrained Phi-3 (DP drops from 0.342 to 0.331; EqOdds from 0.399 to 0.362). These trends hold across models: unlearning consistently reduces fairness gaps, and combining it with CDA amplifies the gains. Notably, unlearned Gemma-2 achieves the best EqOdds (<span id="S4.SSx2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.280</span>) and strong DP (<span id="S4.SSx2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.303</span>), confirming unlearning’s effectiveness across architectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the <span id="S4.SSx2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">German Credit</span> dataset, the <span id="S4.SSx2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM as Feature Extractor</span> setup outperforms both the logistic regression and LLM as Classifier setup across all metrics. Unlearning consistently improves fairness over pretrained models and often surpasses CDA, which tends to reduce accuracy and AccP. Notably, CDA significantly drops the accuracy of Llama-3.1 and Phi-3, while unlearning maintains or improves both fairness and performance. Although trends are less pronounced due to the dataset’s small size and variability, unlearning remains effective.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Fairness and accuracy metrics across three downstream datasets. Models include logistic regression, LLMs as feature extractors, and LLM classifiers. Results are averaged over three train–test splits. <span id="S4.T2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> values indicate the best result per LLM. <span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Color codes indicate mitigation stages: white = no mitigation (Stage 1), red = CDA (Stage 2), blue = unlearning (Stage 3), and purple = combined unlearning + CDA (Stage 4).</span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:505.9pt;height:264.2pt;vertical-align:-130.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-106.3pt,55.5pt) scale(0.704119760224696,0.704119760224696) ;">
<table id="S4.T2.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
