<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this article, we investigate the impact of two mitigation techniques: an adapted unlearning method targeting intrinsic biases, and Counterfactual Data Augmentation addressing extrinsic biases. This section presents the results of our evaluation pipeline and includes an ablation analysis to assess the contribution of each component.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Pipeline Study</h3>

<div id="S5.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results obtained through the different analyses present in the various stages of the pipeline allow us to make the following observations:</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models Performances</h5>

<div id="S5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The use of LLMs yields improvements in accuracy for our classification tasks. Specifically, approaches that leverage LLMs, whether as direct classifiers or as sources of embeddings, consistently outperform feature-based logistic regression. This confirms the utility of LLM-derived representations for feature engineering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Borisov et al. 2022</a>)</cite>. However, for fairness the results of using LLMs without mitigation are more contrasted. Although using LLMs as embeddings directly improves fairness compared to logistic regression, LLMs as classifiers can yield to increasing extrinsic biases in downstream applications depending on the dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SSx1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLMs Differences</h5>

<div id="S5.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe substantial behavioral differences across models depending on the model setup. When used for embedding, Gemma-2 consistently outperforms other models in terms of accuracy, while Llama-3.1, despite its higher theoretical capacity, under-performs. Interestingly, this trend is reversed when considering fairness: Llama-3.1 achieves better fairness metrics, whereas Gemma-2 becomes worse. Phi-3 emerges as a balanced alternative, offering a compromise between accuracy and fairness. Among the LLM-based classifiers, Gemma-2 stands out as the top-performing model, achieving strong results on both accuracy and fairness. In the meantime, Phi-3 exhibits a drop in performance in this configuration, becoming one of the weakest models. Lastly, in the case of the German Credit dataset, Llama-3.1 shows unexpectedly strong performance. This may be attributed to the dataset’s small size, which could lead to overfitting, or the possibility that similar data was included in Llama’s pretraining corpus. This highlights the importance of selecting the appropriate model when performing bias mitigation for downstream applications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SSx1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mitigation Approaches</h5>

<div id="S5.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the biggest improvements on Fairness mainly comes from the use of LLMs, CDA favorably improve DP and Unlearning impact positively Equality of Odds, which is expected. DP requires equal prediction rates across groups regardless of the true label, and CDA supports this by balancing the sensitive attribute distribution, thereby directly influencing group-level predictions. In contrast, Equality of Odds demands equal error rates conditioned on the true outcome. Unlearning operates directly on the model’s internal representations with respect to the true label, making it well-suited to address prediction conditionned on label disparities. Regarding performance trade-offs, CDA often results in decreases in classification accuracy, highlighting its utility cost. Conversely, unlearning does not seem to exhibit such performance degradation. It even leads to improvements in model accuracy and language modeling perplexity, underscoring the promise of in-processing mitigation techniques for achieving fairness without sacrificing downstream task performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S5.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this article, we also introduced a novel unlearning technique that is essential to understand. This ablation study and the analyses on intrinsic bias mitigation reveal interesting dynamics in this unlearning approach, potentially explaining why these methods have been wrongly considered inefficient for downstream applications until now. The detailed ablation results supporting this analysis are presented in Appendix A (Tables 3, 4, and 5)</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">KL Divergence Impact</h5>

<div id="S5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While KL divergence is essential for preserving model coherence, as reflected in perplexity scores, it also preserves the existing intrinsic biases of models. The first factor is that the divergence push model clings to its initial distribution where bias was present. To mitigate this, we attempted to match the distribution of the model on a bias-free dataset as Truthfulqa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin, Hilton, and Evans 2021</a>)</cite>. However, Truthfulqa is not designed to address socio-economic biases, leading the model to preserve biased associations during optimization.
Since LLMs used as classifiers rely heavily on coherence constraints during fine-tuning, this reliance may partly explain their comparatively weaker fairness performance in downstream tasks compared to their embedding classifier counterparts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SSx2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The GAP Loss</h5>

<div id="S5.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The GAP loss plays a critical role in preserving a balanced probability distribution across sensitive attribute values. Without it, we observe that training can lead to a complete inversion of the original distribution, effectively introducing a new form of bias. Moreover, as we can see, flipping distributions is quite effortless since models have an easier time learning anti-stereotyping answers than unlearning stereotyping ones, and thus demand extensive hyperparameter tuning. In cases where balance is not achieved, since the model becomes as efficient at predicting anti-stereotyped answers as it does at predicting stereotyped ones, models may then exploit both the original and the newly introduced flipped bias in LLM representations, resulting in continued unfair predictions in the downstream application. This phenomenon may partly explain why in-processing methods targeting intrinsic bias have been previously underestimated in terms of their downstream impact. Further investigation is required to better understand this effect and its implications in the broader mitigation literature.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SSx2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models and Capacity</h5>

<div id="S5.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across most models, we observe a consistent pattern in which both learning and unlearning collaboratively reduce the gap between biased and unbiased output distributions; up to a point where one mechanism dominates and flips the bias. However, we notice that Llama-3.1 is the only model where learning and unlearning appear to act in contradictory directions with respect to other models. Based on insights from two other LLMs (See Appendix A for details),
we hypothesize that increased model capacity introduces greater representational complexity, making it more difficult to disentangle and remove bias using our current unlearning method. This opposition between learning and unlearning in Llama-3.1 may also explain its behavior in downstream tasks where it diverges from other models by exhibiting inverse trends (e.g., higher fairness but lower accuracy, or vice versa when used as a classifier).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion and Future Direction</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work presents a unified framework for evaluating and mitigating socioeconomic gender bias in large language models (LLMs), addressing both intrinsic (representational) and extrinsic (allocational) dimensions. We adapt concept unlearning, a gradient-based technique originally used for safety, to reduce representational bias without compromising language fluency or downstream task performance. Combined with Counterfactual Data Augmentation (CDA), our method forms a robust dual mitigation strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Empirical results show that unlearning reduces intrinsic bias and enhances fairness in downstream task. For example, in intrinsic evaluation, <span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Phi-3</span>’s gender probability gap dropped from 0.565 to 0.029 (<span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">94.9%</span> reduction). In the <span id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ACS Employment</span> dataset, <span id="S6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unlearned Gemma-2</span> improved Accuracy Parity from 0.199 to 0.104 (<span id="S6.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">48%</span> gain), and <span id="S6.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unlearned Llama-3.1 + CDA</span> reduced Demographic Parity from 0.080 to 0.014 (<span id="S6.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">82%</span> gain). Across four financial datasets, the consistency of these improvements highlights the generalization potential of our pipeline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond mitigation, this work provides a fine-grained lens into how intrinsic and extrinsic biases interact. For users with access to model weights, our framework offers actionable guidance on where mitigation is most effective; for those relying on frozen models, it underscores the importance of early-stage data-level interventions before downstream deployment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While our approach shows strong performance, several limitations remain. First, we focus solely on binary gender bias, omitting other sensitive and intersectional attributes. Second, experiments are limited to English due to the lack of annotated fairness datasets in other languages. Third, our unlearning strategy assumes access to model weights, which restricts its applicability to open-source models. Future work will explore extending this pipeline to multi-attribute, multilingual, and black-box LLM settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We thank Jean‑François Plante for his guidance throughout this work, and Fabrice Normandin for his technical assistance with Mila’s computing infrastructure. Funding support for this research was provided in part by the Canada CIFAR AI Chair, a Google Research Award, the NSERC Discovery Grant, and the IVADO Fundamental Research Projects Grant. We also acknowledge Compute Canada for providing computational resources used in this study.</p>
</div>
</section>
