<li id="bib.bib52" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Yu et al. (2023)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Yu, C.; Jeoung, S.; Kasi, A.; Yu, P.; and Ji, H. 2023.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">Unlearning bias in language models by partitioning gradients.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">In <em id="bib.bib52.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2023</em>, 6032–6048.

</span></li>
    
</ul>
</section>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Ablation Study of Unlearning Loss Components</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We perform an ablation analysis to evaluate the contribution of each loss component in our intrinsic bias mitigation (unlearning) framework. Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Summary. ‣ Appendix A Ablation Study of Unlearning Loss Components ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Summary. ‣ Appendix A Ablation Study of Unlearning Loss Components ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, and Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Summary. ‣ Appendix A Ablation Study of Unlearning Loss Components ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> show the effect of varying the hyperparameters <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{unlearn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>unlearn</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{unlearn}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{learn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>learn</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{learn}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{gap}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{gap}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{norm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>norm</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{norm}}</annotation></semantics></math> on the antistereotype and stereotype probabilities produced by three different models: Llama 3.1, Gemma-2, and Phi-3. Each configuration is evaluated in two contexts (poverty and wealth) to measure how likely the model is to favor stereotypical versus antistereotypical completions under different socioeconomic frames.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results on Llama-3.1.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Summary. ‣ Appendix A Ablation Study of Unlearning Loss Components ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the ablation results for Llama-3.1. The pretrained model exhibits strong bias in the <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">poverty</span> context, where it assigns 69.9% probability to the stereotypical answer, and is nearly balanced in the <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wealth</span> context (50.2% vs. 49.8%). This asymmetry reflects the model’s sensitivity to socioeconomic context even before fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Surprisingly, none of the individual loss components applied in isolation or in combination leads to meaningful bias mitigation. When only learning is used (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{learn}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>learn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{learn}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>), the model shifts only slightly: the antistereotype probability in the poverty context rises from 30.1% to 38.2%. Unlearning alone (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{unlearn}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>unlearn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{unlearn}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) shows a similar limited effect, reducing stereotype preference modestly. Even when learning and unlearning are combined, the change remains marginal, and in some cases, the model appears to revert to the original distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">KL divergence alone (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{norm}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>norm</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{norm}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) entirely preserves the original bias—as expected—and its inclusion in combined loss configurations does not improve fairness. Gap regularization (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{gap}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{gap}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) slightly balances the output probabilities but fails to close the bias gap meaningfully.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These results show that Llama-3.1 exhibits complex and sometimes conflicting dynamics between learning and unlearning components, which may be attributed to its larger capacity and more entangled internal representations. Unlike smaller models, its response to individual loss terms is less predictable, and naïvely combining them does not consistently lead to improved fairness. This highlights the importance of carefully optimizing the hyperparameters. As shown in Table <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, the best combination of loss weights for Llama-3.1 reduces the stereotype–antistereotype probability gap from 0.560 to 0.059, providing strong evidence that effective unlearning is achievable through proper hyperparameter tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results on Gemma-2.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Summary. ‣ Appendix A Ablation Study of Unlearning Loss Components ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents the ablation results for the Gemma-2 model across different combinations of loss components. The pretrained model exhibits clear intrinsic bias: in the <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">poverty</span> context, it assigns 78.7% probability to the stereotype answer, while in the <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wealth</span> context, it slightly favors the antistereotype (56.4%).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When only the antistereotype learning loss is active (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{learn}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>learn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{learn}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>), the model undergoes a dramatic shift, assigning over 97% probability to the antistereotype in both contexts. This suggests that the model can easily learn antistereotypical patterns, but it also results in complete inversion of the original bias—effectively flipping it. In contrast, using only the unlearning loss (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{unlearn}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>unlearn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{unlearn}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) leads to less stable behavior, with probabilities near 50% or still favoring the stereotype. This confirms that unlearning alone is insufficient and must be combined with mechanisms that promote alternative representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The gap regularization loss (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{gap}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{gap}}</annotation></semantics></math>) plays a critical stabilizing role. When used alone, it brings probabilities closer to parity without introducing extreme shifts, and when used in combination, it helps mitigate the bias-flipping effects of strong antistereotype learning. The KL divergence loss (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{norm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>norm</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{norm}}</annotation></semantics></math>), when used alone, tends to preserve the original biased behavior—as expected—since it anchors the model to its pretrained distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, these results demonstrate that while learning is highly effective, it is also prone to flipping the bias if not counterbalanced. The gap loss is particularly important in maintaining distributional balance, while KL regularization constrains generalization. Importantly, simply combining all loss components without careful tuning can lead to suboptimal or conflicting outcomes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results on Phi-3.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Summary. ‣ Appendix A Ablation Study of Unlearning Loss Components ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> presents the ablation study results for the Phi-3 model. The pretrained Phi-3 exhibits a moderate intrinsic bias: in the <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">poverty</span> context, it favors the stereotype with 65.0% probability, while in the <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wealth</span> context, it slightly favors the antistereotype (58.7%). This baseline behavior suggests the model is somewhat sensitive to socioeconomic framing.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When only the learning loss is activated (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{learn}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>learn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{learn}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>), the model shows modest bias reduction, with antistereotype probabilities increasing to 56.1% (poverty) and 51.5% (wealth). Unlike Gemma-2, Phi-3 does not fully flip the bias, indicating a weaker learning response and a more conservative update pattern. Using only the unlearning loss (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{unlearn}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>unlearn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{unlearn}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) leads to a moderate reduction in bias—narrowing the gap between stereotype and antistereotype probabilities, but not eliminating it. For example, in the poverty context, stereotype preference drops from 65.0% to 63.6%.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">KL divergence alone (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{norm}}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>norm</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{norm}}=5</annotation></semantics></math>) essentially preserves the pretrained behavior, as expected. The gap regularization term (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{gap}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{gap}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>), when used in isolation, brings both contexts closer to parity (e.g., 53.2% vs. 46.8%), suggesting it plays a stabilizing role in nudging the model toward balance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, Phi-3 demonstrates more conservative shifts in output distributions compared to Gemma-2. While no single component strongly dominates, the gap loss consistently reduces bias in both directions without overcorrection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Summary.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across all three models, we observe distinct behaviors in response to the unlearning components. Llama-3.1 shows the most resistance to directional updates, with conflicting effects between learning and unlearning likely driven by its greater capacity and representational complexity. This makes hyperparameter tuning especially critical. In contrast, Gemma-2 responds strongly to learning, but also risks flipping the bias unless regularized. Phi-3 demonstrates more moderate shifts, with gap regularization playing a central stabilizing role.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in the main paper, when hyperparameters are carefully optimized, our method can significantly reduce intrinsic bias, for example narrowing the probability gap between stereotype and antistereotype responses from 0.560 to 0.059 in Llama-3.1. These results provide strong evidence that in-processing unlearning methods, when properly tuned, can achieve meaningful and balanced bias mitigation in large language models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Ablation study of loss component weights for intrinsic bias mitigation in Llama-3.1. The <span id="A1.T3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">poverty</span> and <span id="A1.T3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wealth</span> contexts refer to two types of questions in the dataset, representing disadvantaged (e.g., ”Who are poor?”) and privileged (e.g., ”Who are rich?”) socioeconomic scenarios, respectively.</figcaption>
