<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides additional implementation details on evaluation metrics, datasets, models used, and training configurations for both intrinsic bias mitigation and downstream fairness experiments.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Intrinsic Bias Metrics</h3>

<div id="A2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate intrinsic bias, we measure the degree to which the language model assigns unequal probability to stereotype versus antistereotype completions. For each prompt, the model is presented with two candidate answers: one reflecting a stereotypical association, and the other an antistereotypical one.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compute the total log-probability assigned by the model to each answer, then normalize the scores using a softmax over the two options. The intrinsic bias score is defined as the absolute difference in normalized probabilities:</p>
<table id="A2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\left|P_{\text{norm}}(\text{stereotype})-P_{\text{norm}}(\text{antistereotype})\right|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>norm</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>stereotype</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>norm</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>antistereotype</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\left|P_{\text{norm}}(\text{stereotype})-P_{\text{norm}}(\text{antistereotype})\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A larger <math id="A2.SSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> indicates stronger bias — that is, a greater asymmetry in the model’s preference between the two conceptually opposing completions. This metric captures the magnitude of representational bias without assuming either option is more desirable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Extrinsic Bias Metrics</h3>

<div id="A2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our downstream tasks involve classifying individuals based on real-world characteristics, such as salary level, employment status, and creditworthiness. In this setting, <span id="A2.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sex</span> is treated as the sensitive attribute. We define fairness as the requirement that models make predictions equitably, without disproportionately disadvantaging any demographic group.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We report:</p>
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy</span>: Overall classification accuracy across all samples.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy Parity (AccP)</span>: Measures whether prediction accuracy is equal across groups. For example, if accuracy is 85% for males and 70% for females, the absolute difference is 15%, indicating a fairness gap.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
<table id="A2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AccP}=\left|P(\hat{Y}=Y\mid A=0)-P(\hat{Y}=Y\mid A=1)\right|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>AccP</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AccP}=\left|P(\hat{Y}=Y\mid A=0)-P(\hat{Y}=Y\mid A=1)\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.I1.i2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes gender. For instance, if accuracy is 85% for males and 70% for females, the gap is 15%, indicating a fairness violation.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Demographic Parity Difference (DP)</span>: Measures the difference in positive prediction rates between groups:</p>
<table id="A2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{DP}=\left|P(\hat{Y}=1\mid A=0)-P(\hat{Y}=1\mid A=1)\right|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>DP</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{DP}=\left|P(\hat{Y}=1\mid A=0)-P(\hat{Y}=1\mid A=1)\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.I1.i3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes gender. In the Adult dataset, this captures whether men and women are predicted to earn over $50K at similar rates.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Equality of Odds (EqOdds)</span>: Assesses whether the true positive and false positive rates are equal across groups. It is defined as the average of absolute differences across labels:
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
<table id="A2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{EqOdds}=\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>EqOdds</mtext><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{EqOdds}=\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|P(\hat{Y}=1\mid Y{=}0,A{=}0)-P(\hat{Y}=1\mid Y{=}0,A{=}1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|P(\hat{Y}=1\mid Y{=}0,A{=}0)-P(\hat{Y}=1\mid Y{=}0,A{=}1)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|P(\hat{Y}=1\mid Y{=}1,A{=}0)-P(\hat{Y}=1\mid Y{=}1,A{=}1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|P(\hat{Y}=1\mid Y{=}1,A{=}0)-P(\hat{Y}=1\mid Y{=}1,A{=}1)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Datasets</h3>

<div id="A2.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the downstream evaluation, we selected datasets from finance- and employment-related domains that include gender information and exhibit measurable gender disparities.</p>
<ul id="A2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ACS Employment</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. 2021</a>)</cite>: Derived from the American Community Survey (ACS), this dataset is filtered to include individuals aged 16 to 90. While <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. (2021)</a></cite> report that the full dataset shows minimal gender disparity, we select a subset from Utah in 2018, which exhibits a clearer imbalance (e.g., 51% of men employed vs. 41% of women). We use this subsample in our experiments. The filtered subsample contains 22,835 observations and 17 features, and it is used for all experiments.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adult (Census Income)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Becker and Kohavi 1996</a>)</cite>: Also known as the Census Income dataset, this dataset contains 48,842 instances and 14 features such as workclass, hours-per-week, education, and age. The task is to predict whether an individual’s annual income exceeds $50K.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">German Credit</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeh 2009</a>)</cite>: This dataset includes 1,000 instances and 20 attributes. The task is to classify individuals as having either “good” or “bad” credit risk.
This dataset consists of 1,000 instances and 20 features, including checking account status and credit history. The task is to classify credit risk as either “good” or “bad.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.SSx3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Datasets (Intrinsic Bias Evaluation)</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PoorOrRich</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arzaghi, Carichon, and Farnadi 2024</a>)</cite>: Originally designed for masked language modeling, this dataset evaluates associations between demographic groups and socioeconomic descriptors (e.g., poverty, wealth). While the dataset spans multiple demographic axes, we focus exclusively on the gender-related subset. To align it with our unlearning framework, we reformat prompts into a question-answering structure and augment them with more general financial questions, enabling direct comparison with downstream tasks like salary or credit prediction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TruthfulQA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin, Hilton, and Evans 2021</a>)</cite></span> A benchmark for evaluating the truthfulness and factual grounding of language models. In our setup, we use TruthfulQA as a neutral reference distribution to regularize model outputs via KL-divergence, ensuring that unlearning interventions do not degrade overall language model quality.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Language Models</h3>

<div id="A2.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct experiments using three open-source, instruction-tuned families of large language models (LLMs), chosen for their strong performance, manageable size, and compatibility with parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods. All models are accessed through the Hugging Face <span id="A2.SSx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">transformers</span> library.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama-3.1 (8B)</span>: A 8B parameter model trained on approximately 15 trillion tokens from public sources and optimized for multilingual dialogue tasks through instruction tuning and DPO.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. 2024</a>)</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Phi-3 Mini 4K Instruct;</span> A 3.8B parameter, decoder-only Transformer model developed by Microsoft <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdin et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, fine-tuned using Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemma-2-2b-it;</span> A 2B parameter decoder-only model developed by Google <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team 2024</a>)</cite>, optimized for efficiency and aligned with English-language tasks such as reasoning and summarization.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Implementation Details</h2>

<section id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Intrinsic Bias Mitigation.</h5>

<div id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the unlearning step, we conducted a grid search over learning rates <math id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1\text{e}{-5},1\text{e}{-6},1\text{e}{-7}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1\text{e}{-5},1\text{e}{-6},1\text{e}{-7}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and loss weights for each component—unlearning, learning, GAP, and KL divergence—using values in <math id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1.0,0.75,0.5,0.25,0.15\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.75</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1.0,0.75,0.5,0.25,0.15\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each configuration was trained using two NVIDIA A100 80GB GPUs. Model selection was based on validation fairness performance, as measured by intrinsic metrics (probability gap between stereotype and antistereotype completions). Batch size was adjusted for each model based on GPU memory constraints: 12 for Llama-3.1 , 36 for Gemma-2 and Phi-3.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The table below summarizes the best-performing hyperparameter settings for each model:</p>
</div>
<figure id="Ax1.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Best hyperparameter configuration for intrinsic bias mitigation.</figcaption>
<table id="Ax1.T6.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
