</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Downstream Task Evaluation.</h5>

<div id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the downstream fairness experiments, we evaluated models on three classification tasks: S. Each model was used in two modes: (1) as a frozen embedding extractor for training a logistic regression classifier, and (2) as a fine-tuned classifier using LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) for parameter-efficient adaptation. The LoRA configuration was: rank <math id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=16</annotation></semantics></math>, scaling factor <math id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=32</annotation></semantics></math>, dropout = 0.05, and target modules = <math id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\texttt{q\_proj},\texttt{k\_proj},\texttt{v\_proj},\texttt{o\_proj}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">q_proj</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">k_proj</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">v_proj</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">o_proj</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\texttt{q\_proj},\texttt{k\_proj},\texttt{v\_proj},\texttt{o\_proj}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The bias setting was ‘none‘, and the task type was set to ‘causal LM‘.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure robustness, each downstream experiment was conducted using three different train/test splits. For each split, the model was fine-tuned over multiple epochs, and the best-performing checkpoint was selected based on validation accuracy. The selected model was then evaluated on the corresponding test set. We aggregated the results across the three splits and reported the average performance (see Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Extrinsic Bias Evaluation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Intrinsic Meets Extrinsic Fairness: Assessing the Downstream Impact of Bias Mitigation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
